{0 Lightweight database using Ocsipersist}
{%wodoc:aside class="concepts"%}{b Concepts}

Persistent tables with Ocsipersist{%wodoc:end%}

Ocsipersist is a module for persistent references and tables.

For persistent references, Eliom has a higher level interface,
called {e Eliom references}, and you probably want to use it instead
of using Ocsipersist directly.
For example persistent Eliom references of scope session group
can be used to store information about a user (if the session group
corresponds to the user).

Ocsipersist can still be useful for creating persistent key-value tables,
if you do not need the full power of a SQL database.

This tutorial shows how to implement a table for storing users and passwords.

We first create a table ([open_table] returns a promise of the table):
{@ocaml[
let user_table = Ocsipersist.Polymorphic.open_table "user_table"
]}

Then we can easily handle the user management code (with [open Lwt.Syntax],
and the services and the Eliom reference [username] of the
{{!page-interaction}service based Web programming tutorial}):

{@ocaml[
let check_pwd name pwd =
  let* user_table = user_table in
  Lwt.catch
    (fun () ->
      let* saved_password = Ocsipersist.Polymorphic.find user_table name in
      Lwt.return (pwd = saved_password))
    (function Not_found -> Lwt.return false | exn -> Lwt.reraise exn)

let () = Eliom.Registration.Action.register
  ~service:create_account_service
  (fun () (name, pwd) ->
    let* user_table = user_table in
    Ocsipersist.Polymorphic.add user_table name pwd)

let () = Eliom.Registration.Action.register
  ~service:connection_service
  (fun () (name, password) ->
    let* ok = check_pwd name password in
    if ok then Eliom.Reference.set username (Some name) else Lwt.return ())
]}

{e Warning:} for the sake of simplicity, this example stores the
passwords in clear. A real application must store a hash of each
password instead ({{!/ocsigen-start/page-index}Ocsigen Start} does it for you).

{%wodoc:aside class="concept"%}{b Concept: Ocsipersist}


 Ocsipersist implements simple typed key/value tables.
 It can use different backends (SQLite, PostgreSQL or DBM).
 For more information see {!Ocsipersist.table}.
{%wodoc:end%}
